{"name":"Coral Springs","hex":"#abd1af","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Coral Springs","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A city in Broward County, Florida, United States, named for its natural spring water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Coral_Springs"}],"entries":[{"title":"Coral Springs, Florida","blocks":[{"text":"Coral Springs is a city in Broward County, Florida, United States. As of the 2020 census, Coral Springs had a population of 134,394. Approximately 20 miles (32 km) northwest of Fort Lauderdale, it is a principal city of the Miami metropolitan area. It has an arts center, history museum, hosts the \"Our Town\" annual festival, and has Florida's only covered bridge."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Coral_Springs_downtown_January_2019.jpg/330px-Coral_Springs_downtown_January_2019.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coral_Springs%2C_Florida"},{"title":"Coral","blocks":[{"text":"Corals are colonial marine invertebrates within the subphylum Anthozoa of the phylum Cnidaria. They typically form compact colonies of many identical individual polyps. Coral species include the important reef builders that inhabit tropical oceans and secrete calcium carbonate to form a hard skeleton."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Coral_Outcrop_Flynn_Reef.jpg/330px-Coral_Outcrop_Flynn_Reef.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coral"},{"title":"Coral (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The various tones of the color coral are orange, red and pink representations of the colors of those cnidarians known as precious corals."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coral_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Seal_of_Coral_Springs%2C_Florida.png/330px-Seal_of_Coral_Sp